WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.619 --> 00:00:05.609
Like its predecessor, the long-awaited Frozen
sequel is a movie for children, but lots of

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:10.150
grown-ups are still watching it -- and if
anything, Frozen 2 doubles down on bits that

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:12.400
seem aimed squarely at the adults.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.690
Here's a look at some of the most memorable
moments only grown-ups noticed.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.020
This is it, the moment you've been waiting
for.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.910
Elsa finally, officially comes out of the
closet!

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:26.480
Just kidding, she definitely doesn't, but
Frozen 2 does throw in a scene that seems

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:31.750
to hint that Elsa could possibly be gay, even
if it mainly just comes across as Disney throwing

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:35.609
a bone to fans who were hoping for some concrete
proof of her sexuality.

00:00:35.609 --> 00:00:39.899
In the film, Elsa and company venture to an
enchanted forest in the hopes of lifting a

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:44.750
curse that has left the Northuldra and an
Arendellian army unit trapped in the forest

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.589
for over 30 years.

00:00:46.589 --> 00:00:51.329
While staying with the Northuldra, Elsa sits
by a fire with a young Northuldra woman named

00:00:51.329 --> 00:00:52.329
Honeymaren.

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:56.159
The two have a conversation about the spirits
and the forest, and they learn that they share

00:00:56.159 --> 00:01:00.269
a bit of history thanks to a song Elsa's mother
sang to her when she was young.

00:01:00.269 --> 00:01:04.790
Their interaction comes across as a very flirty
meet-cute, and while it will fly right over

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:09.580
kids' heads, any adult can interpret this
as Disney's very safe attempt at trying to

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.190
hint at Elsa's sexuality.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.760
Ultimately, their interaction goes nowhere
and they don't speak again for the rest of

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:18.800
the film, making the tension-filled scene
something of a disappointment for the fans

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.570
who were hoping for Elsa's big coming out
party.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:26.300
The Marvel Cinematic Universe has been a major
cash cow for Disney.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:30.170
Naturally, this sort of success has started
to influence the rest of the studio's offerings,

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:33.890
as everyone wants a bit of that MCU magic
for themselves.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:36.170
And Frozen 2 is no exception.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:40.910
Very early in the movie, there is a big battle
scene between the Arendelle army and the Northuldra.

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:45.040
This is followed by a ton of action scenes
— far more than the first Frozen film.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:50.570
Frozen 2 is practically an action movie, and
most of the action feels very big and MCU-esque.

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:51.980
Then there's the direct reference.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.860
Near the end of the film, Elsa becomes frozen
solid.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:58.050
As a result, any magic she created begins
to disappear.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:02.250
This includes Olaf, who begins "flurrying"
— as in, pieces of snow start flaking off

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.730
of him and floating away.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:08.280
It looks exactly like the dusting that occurs
in Avengers: Infinity War, and at one point

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:13.819
Anna even cradles the disintegrating Olaf
in her arms just as Iron Man cradled Spider-Man.

00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:20.629
You half-expect Olaf to say, "I don't feel
so good, Mr. Stark" before he disappears completely.

00:02:20.629 --> 00:02:24.540
One of the film's big reveals is that the
Arendellian soldiers and the Northuldra who

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:29.489
engaged in a battle 34 years prior are still
living in the enchanted forest.

00:02:29.489 --> 00:02:33.189
That's pretty incredible, both that they went
that long without killing each other and also

00:02:33.189 --> 00:02:36.989
that they were able to find enough food to
stay alive for over three decades.

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:39.790
But their survival isn't the most impressive
thing about this ordeal.

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:43.260
No, that would be the Arendellian army's clothes.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:47.299
When Elsa and company meet Mattias and the
rest of the soldiers, they're all still wearing

00:02:47.299 --> 00:02:48.299
their uniforms.

00:02:48.299 --> 00:02:51.140
You know, the uniforms they were wearing 34
years ago.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:54.599
That implies that they have no other clothes,
which means they've worn the same thing every

00:02:54.599 --> 00:02:59.040
day for decades, and they're still as crisp
and clean as the day Arendelle marched off

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.040
to battle.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:03.569
Kids will pay little attention to this, but
adults might wonder where they can invest

00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:06.859
in some of those most durable duds.

00:03:06.859 --> 00:03:11.319
We don't know exactly what Anna and Kristoff
get up to in the bedroom, but Frozen 2 makes

00:03:11.319 --> 00:03:15.330
it clear that the new queen of Arendelle has
at least one major kink.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:19.829
At the end of the film, some of the characters
dress up in some fancy duds to celebrate Anna's

00:03:19.829 --> 00:03:20.829
coronation.

00:03:20.829 --> 00:03:23.870
The clothes look great, but Kristoff hates
them, finding them uncomfortable.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.540
"You get this for one hour," Kristoff tells
Anna.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:27.940
"That's okay," Anna purrs.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:29.730
"I prefer you in leather anyway."

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.849
*gigantic spit-take*

00:03:31.849 --> 00:03:34.120
Anna, you naughty girl.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:37.669
While kids will think that Anna is simply
making a cute reference to Kristoff's regular

00:03:37.669 --> 00:03:42.569
leather jerkin, their parents will get the
joke: When the lights go out, Anna likes to

00:03:42.569 --> 00:03:43.849
get a little freaky.

00:03:43.849 --> 00:03:45.260
"This is awkward."

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.989
That's not the only racy comment in the scene,
either.

00:03:47.989 --> 00:03:52.010
After the exchange, Olaf — who's similarly
dressed up — remarks, "I'm shocked you can

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.010
last an hour."

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:54.579
And if you don't get that one…

00:03:54.579 --> 00:03:57.609
well, ask your parents, maybe.

00:03:57.609 --> 00:04:02.170
Hamilton and Glee star Jonathan Groff, who
voices Kristoff, didn't get a solo song in

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:05.650
the original Frozen, but Frozen 2 made it
up to him.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:10.379
In the sequel, Groff belts out "Lost in the
Woods," one of the film's most memorable tracks.

00:04:10.379 --> 00:04:14.621
Sure, Groff's performance is unsurprisingly
spectacular, but what really makes "Lost in

00:04:14.621 --> 00:04:16.720
the Woods" shine is its production.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:19.590
The number doesn't just evoke old '80s power
ballads.

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:24.319
It's also staged exactly like something you
would've seen on classic MTV, with a few shots

00:04:24.319 --> 00:04:28.069
ripped directly from Queen's "Bohemian Rhapsody"
thrown in for good measure.

00:04:28.069 --> 00:04:33.150
The whole thing is outlandish enough to be
funny for everyone, but only adults will understand

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.470
what "Lost in the Woods" is parodying.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:36.470
That's by design.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.849
In an interview with Entertainment Weekly,
Groff says,

00:04:38.849 --> 00:04:41.690
"It's... really kind of a gift for the adults
watching the movie....

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:46.169
The kids are laughing too, but the adults
are the ones that are really in on the joke."

00:04:46.169 --> 00:04:50.550
Yes, it's true: in Frozen 2, Elsa and Anna
wear pants.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:53.509
As co-director Jennifer Lee points out, that
makes sense.

00:04:53.509 --> 00:04:55.780
As the filmmaker put it to the Los Angeles
Times,

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:59.810
"Of course you're going to wear pants when
you hear the words 'enchanted forest.'"

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:03.520
Frozen 2 is a rough-and-tumble adventure,
and its heroes are princesses.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.940
They should be allowed to wear whatever they
want.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:10.080
And yet, over the course of Disney's history,
that hasn't always been the case.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:15.050
Aside from Mulan and Aladdin's Princess Jasmine,
every other Disney princess has been confined

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:16.870
to dresses or skirts.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:21.069
Even Anna and Elsa were stuck in long, flowing
gowns in the first Frozen, and only got to

00:05:21.069 --> 00:05:23.870
wear more practical attire when the sequel
rolled around.

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:27.680
According to Walt Disney Animation Studios
president Clark Spencer, switching up the

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:32.750
duo's wardrobe was a way to bring modern sensibilities
into a fairytale world, and a proclamation

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:33.750
that

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:36.030
"...there is more than one way to be a princess."

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:37.919
Kids probably won't notice the change.

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:40.550
Those of us who grew up on classic Disney
features, though?

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:43.539
We know it's a big deal.

00:05:43.539 --> 00:05:48.050
Frozen 2 is full of nods to other movies — especially
other Disney films — but the movie's target

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:50.510
audience is going to miss at least one of
them.

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:54.750
Do you know many children who are familiar
with The Seven Year Itch, the 1955 comedy

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:56.460
starring Marilyn Monroe?

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:57.880
Yeah, we didn't think so.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:01.610
Disney's animators snuck in a sly reference
to the film anyway.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:05.830
The Seven Year Itch is the movie in which
Monroe stands over a subway grate as wind

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.280
from a passing train lifts her white dress.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.789
It's one of the most memorable images in film
history.

00:06:10.789 --> 00:06:13.409
Well, Frozen 2 has a similar scene.

00:06:13.409 --> 00:06:17.470
When the wind spirit Gale is playing with
Olaf, the snowman's bottom half billows out

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:19.199
just like Monroe's outfit.

00:06:19.199 --> 00:06:23.220
Olaf even calls the experience "delicious,"
echoing the line that Monroe utters during

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:24.659
the iconic scene.

00:06:24.659 --> 00:06:29.500
"Oh, do you feel the breeze from the subway?

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.120
Isn't it delicious?"

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:34.810
It's too much to be a coincidence — and
it's a reference that's going to go right

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:36.570
over most kids' heads.

